[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Enterprise Messaging and Event-Driven Architecture in .NET with MassTransit, RabbitMQ, and Apache Kafka"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern enterprise applications often require distributed architectures where services communicate asynchronously. Event-Driven Architecture (EDA) decouples producers from consumers, improving scalability and resilience. In the .NET ecosystem, MassTransit acts as a powerful distributed application framework that abstracts underlying message brokers like RabbitMQ and Apache Kafka."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts: Messaging vs. Event Streaming"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before diving into implementation, it is essential to distinguish between traditional message queuing and event streaming. RabbitMQ is a traditional message broker optimized for complex routing, task distribution, and point-to-point or publish-subscribe messaging where messages are removed after consumption. Apache Kafka is a distributed event streaming platform designed for high-throughput, log-based storage where events are retained for a specified duration and can be replayed by multiple consumers."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Up MassTransit with RabbitMQ"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"RabbitMQ is ideal for transactional messaging and command-query separation. To get started, install the MassTransit.RabbitMQ NuGet package. Below is how you configure MassTransit in a .NET Generic Host (.NET 8/9):"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"builder.Services.AddMassTransit(x =>
{
    x.UsingRabbitMq((context, cfg) =>
    {
        cfg.Host("localhost", "/", h =>
        {
            h.Username("guest");
            h.Password("guest");
        });

        cfg.ConfigureEndpoints(context);
    });
});"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Contracts and Consumers"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Messages in MassTransit are defined using simple .NET interfaces or classes. It is a best practice to share these contracts in a common library between publisher and consumer services."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public interface OrderSubmitted
{
    Guid OrderId { get; }
    DateTime Timestamp { get; }
}

public class OrderSubmittedConsumer : IConsumer<OrderSubmitted>
{
    private readonly ILogger<OrderSubmittedConsumer> _logger;

    public OrderSubmittedConsumer(ILogger<OrderSubmittedConsumer> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public async Task Consume(ConsumeContext<OrderSubmitted> context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Processing Order ID: {OrderId}", context.Message.OrderId);
        // Add business logic here
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Integrating Apache Kafka with MassTransit"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"When switching to an event-streaming model using Kafka, MassTransit provides integration via the MassTransit.Kafka package. Kafka relies on topics and producer/consumer topologies rather than exchanges and queues."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"builder.Services.AddMassTransit(x =>
{
    x.UsingRabbitMq((context, cfg) =>
    {
        cfg.ConfigureEndpoints(context);
    });

    x.AddRider(rider =>
    {
        rider.AddProducer<OrderSubmitted>("order-submitted-topic");

        rider.UsingKafka((context, k) =>
        {
            k.Host("localhost:9092");

            k.TopicEndpoint<Guid, OrderSubmitted>("order-submitted-topic", "order-group", e =>
            {
                e.ConfigureConsumer<OrderSubmittedConsumer>(context);
            });
        });
    });
});"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Publishing Messages"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"To publish messages or events within your application controllers or background services, inject the IPublishEndpoint or harness Kafka producers directly."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class OrdersController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly IPublishEndpoint _publishEndpoint;

    public OrdersController(IPublishEndpoint publishEndpoint)
    {
        _publishEndpoint = publishEndpoint;
    }

    [HttpPost]
    public async Task<IActionResult> SubmitOrder()
    {
        var orderId = Guid.NewGuid();
        await _publishEndpoint.Publish<OrderSubmitted>(new
        {
            OrderId = orderId,
            Timestamp = DateTime.UtcNow
        });

        return Accepted(new { OrderId = orderId });
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Resilient Architectures"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implement Fault Tolerance: Configure retry policies and Dead Letter Queues (DLQ) in MassTransit to handle transient network errors safely."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Idempotency: Ensure your consumers handle duplicate messages gracefully by tracking processed message IDs in a database."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Contract Evolution: Avoid breaking changes in event contracts. Use optional properties or versioned namespaces when payloads change."}]
